    "body": "Adds ADR-016, which decides how inline elements flow with surrounding text and how paragraph direction is resolved.\n\n## The gaps this fixes\n\n**Inline formatting**: as of v0.3.1, `<p>Hello <em>world</em>!</p>` renders as three vertically stacked blocks. Firefox renders it as one flowing line with `world` emphasized. That is the biggest structural gap remaining in the renderer -- real HTML uses inline elements constantly.\n\n**RTL heuristic divergence**: ADR-015 shipped a first-strong heuristic that applies unconditionally. The welcome page's fourth line (mixed Arabic then English) renders right-aligned in HoneyBee but left-aligned in Firefox, because Firefox respects the inherited direction (default LTR) while HoneyBee applies the heuristic per text node. That divergence is documented in docs/current-state.md under \"Known behavior: RTL direction heuristic\". ADR-016 provides the real fix: dir attribute support.\n\n## The decision\n\nThree coordinated changes, bundled because they interlock:\n\n1. display property (block, inline; none/flex/grid deferred). Read from ComputedStyle::get(\"display\") in layout. Default block when missing (safer than HTML's inline default).\n\n2. Inline formatting context. Block containers collect inline children into a LayoutInlineContext (a synthetic block), flatten the inline tree depth-first into (text, style) items, and run line breaking across the whole sequence. That is what allows \"Hello world!\" to flow together and wrap correctly. LineFragment gains style fields so fragments from an em or span carry their own font/color.\n\n3. dir attribute support (ltr, rtl, auto). Resolved per block container. ltr/rtl explicit; auto applies the heuristic; missing inherits from parent (default LTR at html). This makes the ADR-015 heuristic opt-in rather than forced. The welcome page's mixed paragraph, with no dir, now inherits LTR and renders left-aligned -- matching Firefox.\n\n## Why bundled, not split into two ADRs\n\n- display: inline without an inline formatting context is dead code\n- Inline context without dir support inherits ADR-015's divergence\n- Both changes touch the same code paths (layout.cpp, text_utils.cpp, paint.cpp)\n- ADR-017 immediately following ADR-016 would churn the same files\n\nThe implementation will be 5-6 commits (display property, inline node types, inline context construction, direction resolution, paint per-fragment style, tests). The ADR is the design; the commits break it into reviewable pieces.\n\n## Scope\n\nReads 353 lines. Defers: display none/flex/grid/table, baseline alignment, direction CSS property, full UAX #9 bidi, inline across block boundaries (fallback to block), vertical-align, line-height, float, position.\n\nUA stylesheet gains 19 display: inline rules (span, a, em, i, strong, b, code, kbd, samp, var, small, sub, sup, abbr, cite, q, s, u, time, mark).\n\n## Consequences\n\n- src/renderer/layout/layout_box.hpp -- Display enum, LayoutKind::Inline, LayoutInline, LayoutInlineContext, LineFragment style fields\n- src/renderer/layout/layout.cpp -- display reading, inline run collection, inline context, dir attribute resolution\n- src/renderer/layout/text_utils.cpp -- per-word style in line breaking\n- src/renderer/paint/paint.cpp -- per-fragment style painting\n- src/renderer/css/ua_stylesheet.cpp -- 19 display: inline rules\n- Tests updated in layout, paint, ua_stylesheet\n\nNo changes to renderer::dom, renderer::css (except UA content), renderer::html.\n\n## Behavior examples\n\n- p Hello em world /em ! -> 1 line \"Hello world!\" (was 3 stacked blocks)\n- p dir=ltr with Arabic -> left-aligned (was right-aligned by heuristic)\n- html dir=rtl with Arabic -> right-aligned (inherited)\n- p dir=auto with Arabic -> right-aligned (opt-in heuristic)\n- p with div inside -> block + anonymous inline block (matches Firefox)\n\n## Next\n\nOnce ADR-016 merges, the implementation issue will be \"Implement inline formatting, display, and dir attribute per ADR-016\" -- 5-6 commits, same rhythm as ADR-015's implementation. This is the last major structural gap before pages start looking like pages that load their own CSS.\n",
